<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoleon III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napoleon III, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and then Emperor of the French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> from 1852 to 1870. He built specialised colonial troops for overseas campaigns. According to the French Empire profile, French territorial holdings tripled during his reign, which ended with defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="Napoleon III" width="300" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre Cabanel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Cabanel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles-Louis Napoléon Bonaparte was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on the night of 19–20 April 1808, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>, King of Holland, and Hortense de Beauharnais. At fifteen he moved with his mother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he and his brother Napoléon-Louis joined the Carbonari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> opposed to Austrian domination of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>When the Duke of Reichstadt, Napoleon I's son, died in 1832, Louis Napoleon became the de facto heir of the dynasty and leader of the Bonapartist cause. On 6 August 1840 he sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> with about sixty armed men in an attempt to overthrow King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a>. It failed, and he was held in the fortress of Ham for six years, which damaged his health.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the February Revolution of 1848 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and was elected president of the Second Republic on 10–11 December 1848. Barred from re-election, he carried out a coup d'état on 1–2 December 1851: soldiers occupied key points in Paris, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> was dissolved and a rising on 3 December was crushed, with an estimated 300 to 400 opponents killed.</p><p>The constitution of 1852 gave him unlimited ten-year terms and the authority to declare war, sign treaties and initiate laws, and he became Emperor. His government built railways, ports and canals, modernised banking, and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-eugene-haussmann">Georges-Eugène Haussmann</a> rebuild central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The Cobden–Chevalier Treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of 23 January 1860 lowered tariffs, and workers gained the right to strike.</p><p>In foreign policy he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> of 1854–1856 and fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, receiving Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> in 1860. The attempt to create a Mexican empire under Archduke Maximilian ended in failure, and French troops withdrew in 1866 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> pressed for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1870 Napoleon III reluctantly declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> after pressure from the public. Despite failing health he took command of the army, which was rapidly defeated, and he was captured at Sedan. He was held at Schloss Wilhelmshöhe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> from 5 September 1870 to 19 March 1871, while the Third Republic was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He went into exile in the United Kingdom and died there on 9 January 1873.</p><p>His reputation has suffered from the failures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> called him Napoleon the Small, and historians have often seen him as an authoritarian but ineffectual leader. His government also continued the preservation of medieval buildings under Prosper Mérimée and Eugène Viollet-le-Duc, among them Notre Dame Cathedral, Mont Saint-Michel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Napoleon III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lord John Russell, <b>Napoleon III</b> and Gladstone toyed with recognition or mediation until Antietam and the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation of September 1862 ended it; British opinion, proud of ending the slave trade and taught by Frederick Douglass and other Black abolitionists, was against the Confederacy except in Liverpool.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Napoleon III</b> spoke of an “Arab kingdom”, but his sénatus-consulte of 1865 made Muslims French subjects who could become citizens only by renouncing Islamic personal status, which very few did.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Napoleon III</b>, colonial campaigns added Cochinchina, New Caledonia, and an expanded Senegal under Louis Faidherbe, and the Third Republic later pushed into Tunisia, Tonkin, Madagascar, and the Sahel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franco-Prussian War of 1870–1871 brought down <b>Napoleon III</b> and his Second French Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1867 <b>Napoleon III</b> of France negotiated to buy the grand duchy from William III of the Netherlands, provoking the Luxembourg Crisis with Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with <b>Napoleon III</b> of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained Lombardy; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Chislehurst, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Napoleon III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
